WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:17.850
Miss Dior: Resistance fighter, Ravensbruck
survivor

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:22.630
Christian Dior is one of the most iconic names
in fashion — so iconic that even those with

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:24.720
zero fashion sense have heard of him.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:29.930
His most popular line is arguably the equally
famous Miss Dior, and while everyone's heard

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:35.440
of the floral perfume, not everyone knows
that there was a very real Miss Dior — and

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.370
her first name was Catherine.

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:42.310
The perfume was launched in 1947, and when
Christian Dior wrote his autobiography just

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:47.250
a few years later, he said the name came to
him in a moment of inspiration, when he heard

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:52.070
his mentor greet his sister with the words,
"Voila, Miss Dior!"

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:53.690
In a way, it was a fitting honor.

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:57.420
Catherine was, by all accounts, one of her
brother's biggest supporters.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:01.320
But on the other hand, she was perhaps the
last person one might think would lend their

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:02.399
name to a perfume.

00:01:02.399 --> 00:01:08.459
Her contemporaries knew her more for her unconventional
nature and, when push came to shove, her fighting

00:01:08.459 --> 00:01:09.459
spirit.

00:01:09.459 --> 00:01:14.369
She was born in 1917, and as a young woman
living in France during World War Two, well,

00:01:14.369 --> 00:01:17.310
that fight and determination served her well.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:21.680
By the time the war came to a close, she would
become a highly decorated member of the French

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:27.689
Resistance, even though she would spend those
final, dark days as a prisoner in a concentration

00:01:27.689 --> 00:01:28.779
camp.

00:01:28.779 --> 00:01:35.079
A garden of peace before the horrors of war

00:01:35.079 --> 00:01:39.640
The lives and work of both Christian Dior
and his sister would have one consistent theme:

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:43.200
a love of flowers and gardening, garnered
from their mother, Madelaine.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.299
The family's early years were peaceful, idyllic,
even.

00:01:46.299 --> 00:01:50.729
The Dior patriarch had provided well for his
growing family, accumulating a certain amount

00:01:50.729 --> 00:01:52.990
of wealth as a producer of fertilizer.

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:56.619
They were well-off enough to keep more than
one home, but the one they remembered most

00:01:56.619 --> 00:02:01.489
fondly was a family home called Villa Les
Rhumbs in Granville, a beautiful seaside resort

00:02:01.489 --> 00:02:02.840
town in Normandy.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:08.119
That was just a second home by the time Catherine
was born in 1917, and for most of the time,

00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:09.629
the family lived in Paris.

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:15.100
Still, the young Dior siblings had their childhood
shaped by the extensive gardens and flowers

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:20.019
they remembered their mother patiently growing
and cultivating, an influence that would become

00:02:20.019 --> 00:02:21.709
evident in later years.

00:02:21.709 --> 00:02:25.939
Not all good things seem to last, though,
and it was certainly true for the Diors.

00:02:25.939 --> 00:02:31.099
Christian, in particular, was of a superstitious
nature, and would later recall the very instant

00:02:31.099 --> 00:02:36.680
the tide of their lives changes: the moment
that a mirror fell to the floor in their empty

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.620
house, and shattered.

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:44.040
"Misfortune came into our hitherto happy and
sheltered family immediately," he would write.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:49.049
One of their brothers grew ill, and their
mother "suddenly faded away and died of grief,"

00:02:49.049 --> 00:02:50.110
Christian would remember.

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:54.780
Brother Bernard would be committed to an institution,
where he would live until his death in 1960,

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:57.840
and suddenly, the wealth they had long enjoyed
was gone.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.890
The Dior patriarch lost everything in the
Stock Market Crash of 1929, Christian's modernist

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:04.409
art gallery went bankrupt, and he was diagnosed
with tuberculosis.

00:03:04.409 --> 00:03:09.219
Amidst it all, he found himself caring for
his siblings — including his favorite, Catherine.

00:03:09.219 --> 00:03:14.080
The family moved to a small piece of property
in Callian, an area appropriately well-known

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:17.470
for growing the flowers that supply France's
perfume industry.

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:21.900
It was there that Christian began to draw,
selling his sketches to help support his family

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:25.790
and finally heading to Paris to enter the
design world for the first time.

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:29.499
Catherine followed him in 1936, and worked
at a nearby boutique.

00:03:29.499 --> 00:03:34.920
It was their first foray into the adult world,
into the realm of Parisian fashion and industry.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.189
But again, the good times were not to last.

00:03:38.189 --> 00:03:43.060
War was lingering on the edge of everyone's
minds, and by 1939, businesses were closing

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:46.909
as people prepared for what they knew was
inevitable conflict.

00:03:46.909 --> 00:03:50.780
Christian was conscripted into the military,
and Catherine returned to her father and Callian.

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:55.300
Her brother joined them fairly quickly after
he was demobilized in 1940, and here again

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:56.709
their lives intertwine.

00:03:56.709 --> 00:04:01.319
They grew vegetables, helped supplement the
meagerness of wartime supplies, and made regular

00:04:01.319 --> 00:04:06.359
excursions into Cannes, where they were kept
apprised of all that was going on in the war..

00:04:06.359 --> 00:04:10.909
They were on the outskirts of the conflict,
but they would not stay there for long.

00:04:10.909 --> 00:04:18.060
French Resistance, a chance meeting, and a
married man

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:23.000
On June 14, 1940, the day that so many Parisian
businesses had been preparing for finally

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:24.000
happened.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.300
Suddenly, the city was under a strict 8pm
curfew, and German troops were announcing

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:29.840
their official occupation.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:34.199
An estimated 2 million people had already
left ahead of the impending invasion, and

00:04:34.199 --> 00:04:37.190
for those that remained, life was… complicated.

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:42.130
Parisians needed to find a way to co-exist
with their occupiers, and some — like Paris's

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:46.410
other world-famous fashion designer, Coco
Chanel — decided the best way to get through

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:49.030
the war was to collaborate with the German
military.

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:53.570
She dated a high-ranking member of the Abwehr,
moved into the Paris Hotel Ritz, and was given

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:58.431
her own code name even as she used the ongoing
persecution of the Jews to renegotiate some

00:04:58.431 --> 00:04:59.431
business deals.

00:04:59.431 --> 00:05:02.520
Catherine and Christian Dior did the opposite.

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:05.840
In 1941, Christian headed back to Paris, leaving
his sister at Callian.

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:11.500
He secured a position at the fashion house
of Lucien Lelong, and Lelong did an incredible

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:12.500
thing.

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:16.169
Even as Nazi officers informed him they were
going to be moving all of Paris's fashion

00:05:16.169 --> 00:05:21.500
industry to Berlin, Lelong first argued that
it just wasn't possible to move the thousands

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:23.340
of skilled artisans to another city.

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:27.949
You couldn't retrain them, either, as it took
decades to become as skillful as his employees

00:05:27.949 --> 00:05:28.949
were.

00:05:28.949 --> 00:05:31.580
So, he argued… fashion needed to stay in
Paris.

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:36.500
Germany relented, the fashion industry remained
in Paris, and Lelong saved thousands of industry

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:39.540
workers — many of whom were Jewish refugees.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:44.419
Even as Christian established himself in the
industry as one of Lelong's most valued employees,

00:05:44.419 --> 00:05:46.150
Catherine was back in Callian.

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:51.139
It was the same year she did a seemingly innocuous
thing that would change her life.

00:05:51.139 --> 00:05:56.110
It was November of 1941 and she went shopping
for a radio in a store in Cannes.

00:05:56.110 --> 00:05:59.889
It was on that perfectly ordinary shopping
trip that she met a man she would fall in

00:05:59.889 --> 00:06:00.889
love with.

00:06:00.889 --> 00:06:05.870
His name was Herve des Charbonneries, and
he came with, well, some baggage.

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:08.860
He was married at the time, to the mother
of his three children.

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:12.650
Lucie — who was rumored to have had plenty
of affairs of her own — didn't matter, though,

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:17.009
not when it came to the blossoming love between
Herve and the young Catherine.

00:06:17.009 --> 00:06:21.610
It was more than a simple wartime fling, though:
he and Catherine didn't just fall in love,

00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:24.949
they joined a British-sponsored section of
the French Resistance.

00:06:24.949 --> 00:06:29.379
It was called the Famille, or F2, and it was
cooperative intelligence network that had

00:06:29.379 --> 00:06:34.080
been set up by French and Polish intelligence
officers in the summer of 1940, with some

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:35.289
help from the British.

00:06:35.289 --> 00:06:39.599
She officially joined on July 1, 1943, just
a few months after Lucie.

00:06:39.599 --> 00:06:43.139
It was dangerous work — it was the first
branch of the French Resistance devoted to

00:06:43.139 --> 00:06:47.060
gathering intelligence, and they were doing
it under the nose of a Vichy government so

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:52.250
sympathetic to the Nazi cause of extermination
that they didn't just comply with the Nazis

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:57.169
— they went above and beyond to identify
members of their Jewish population.

00:06:57.169 --> 00:07:01.349
Catherine became an intelligence agent with
the Massif Central unit; most of her work

00:07:01.349 --> 00:07:06.229
was gathering and passing along information
on the movement of Germany's troops and weapons.

00:07:06.229 --> 00:07:10.050
Reports were sent to London, to the Allies,
and to the troops who were under the command

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:15.300
of General De Gaulle; Catherine, now 29 years
old but looking a decade younger, spent much

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:19.580
of her time of her bicycle, attracting the
sort of attention a pretty young girl riding

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:24.189
along through the French countryside would
tend to get from the men she cycled past.

00:07:24.189 --> 00:07:29.229
It was a nearly ideal cover, and it allowed
her to travel throughout the area fairly freely.

00:07:29.229 --> 00:07:33.319
When she was in Paris, she would often stay
with her brother at his apartment, and even

00:07:33.319 --> 00:07:37.949
as close as they were, it's unclear whether
or not she ever told him what she was doing.

00:07:37.949 --> 00:07:42.110
It was, after all, the sort of thing he was
better off not knowing about — especially

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:46.469
considering he spent his days designing dresses
for the wives of the Third Reich.

00:07:46.469 --> 00:07:50.740
Safety was, of course, first and foremost,
and if she didn't tell him, it was a secret

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:52.430
she kept to protect him.

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:56.350
The stakes were high, and to say what she
was doing was a matter of life and death is

00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:57.350
no exaggeration.

00:07:57.350 --> 00:08:01.460
In later memoirs, Catherine would remember
one day in particular that she met a friend

00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:05.000
— and fellow resistance member — Liliane
Dietlin at a cafe.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:08.780
She hopped off her bike and hugged her friend,
tightly enough that Dietlin remembered that

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:10.780
moment even 35 years later.

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:13.700
She asked Catherine about it, and Catherine
remembered, too.

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:17.409
She had delivered four messages that day,
three in the morning and one in the afternoon.

00:08:17.409 --> 00:08:19.409
Eight people had been arrested.

00:08:19.409 --> 00:08:21.979
Most would be tortured, and all would be executed.

00:08:21.979 --> 00:08:27.550
When asked if there were many days like that,
she responded with a sad shrug and simply

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:28.979
said, "Ah, oui."

00:08:28.979 --> 00:08:31.199
It wasn't just the enemy they knew about,
either.

00:08:31.199 --> 00:08:35.590
By the middle of 1944, counter-intelligence
officers loyal to the Germans and to the Vichy

00:08:35.590 --> 00:08:37.690
government had infiltrated F2.

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:39.380
Suddenly, no one could be trusted.

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:43.940
Hundreds of French Resistance agents were
arrested, torture was common, and Catherine

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:48.560
would have seen the bodies of her allies recovered
from where they had been dumped in the streets.

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:50.540
July was a particularly bad month.

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:54.770
Dozens of agents were arrested, including
Jean Desbordes., who went by the code name

00:08:54.770 --> 00:08:55.770
Duroc.

00:08:55.770 --> 00:09:00.020
He was taken on July 5, and held for four
days of torture before his body was recovered.

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:03.410
He had been beaten to death, his eyes torn
from his head.

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:25.740
Catherine was arrested on July 6.

00:09:25.740 --> 00:10:44.100
From haute couture to concentration camp

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:48.020
When she showed up in Trocadero Square, she
thought she was keeping an appointment she'd

00:10:48.020 --> 00:10:50.380
made with a colleague in the resistance network.

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:54.330
Instead, she was met by police under the direction
of a German French Foreign Legion veteran

00:10:54.330 --> 00:10:55.740
named Friedrich Berger.

00:10:55.740 --> 00:11:00.020
Berger had set up a base for his operations
in a wealthy Parisian neighborhood, and his

00:11:00.020 --> 00:11:05.200
unit of intelligence officers were known for
such brutal torture that Allied officers estimated

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:10.490
that the longest their most hardened, well-trained
operatives could hold out was 24 hours.

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:14.370
Catherine was in custody for several weeks,
and when other prisoners were asked what had

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:20.350
happened behind the closed doors at 180 Rue
de le Pompe, they said that she never broke.

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:24.590
Berger's unit was so efficient at uncovering
French Resistance members that it wasn't long

00:11:24.590 --> 00:11:29.380
before Catherine needed to be moved to another
secure holding facility to await transport

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:31.070
to her final destination.

00:11:31.070 --> 00:11:36.030
On July 27, she was taken to the nearby Fresnes
prison — and this is a case of a few days

00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:37.630
making all the difference in the world.

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:42.990
Berger and his men would flee Paris on August
17, and before they left, they shot and killed

00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:45.270
the 44 prisoners they were holding.

00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:48.060
Christian, meanwhile, heard about his sister's
arrest.

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:51.800
Even with the contacts he'd made through the
fashion house, he wasn't able to get anyone

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:54.490
to help secure her release… until it was
too late.

00:11:54.490 --> 00:11:59.950
On August 15 — just 10 days before the liberation
of Paris — Catherine was forced onto a hot,

00:11:59.950 --> 00:12:03.080
overcrowded train with more than 600 other
women.

00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:09.070
Their final destination was Germany, and in
this case, time was not on her side.

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:12.950
Christian had succeeded in getting through
to the Swedish Consul General located in Paris,

00:12:12.950 --> 00:12:15.990
and they were willing to speak to the Germans
on her behalf.

00:12:15.990 --> 00:12:20.320
Raoul Nordling had already made some impressive
strides: he had already convinced German authorities

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:24.610
not to carry out Hitler's orders to destroy
the city, and he had secured the transfer

00:12:24.610 --> 00:12:27.060
of political prisoners into Swedish custody.

00:12:27.060 --> 00:12:30.340
And Catherine Dior was very nearly included
in that deal.

00:12:30.340 --> 00:12:34.980
She was on the train, carrying a suffocating
stench of death that was so strong, the guards

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:37.220
riding on the roof couldn't stand it.

00:12:37.220 --> 00:12:40.780
Nordling got permission to get her off the
train, as long as he could get a phone call

00:12:40.780 --> 00:12:44.960
through to the SS officers when they stopped
at the station in Bar-le-Duc.

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:48.310
He couldn't get through, and the train kept
going.

00:12:48.310 --> 00:12:52.930
Catherine was routed to Ravensbruck, where
she was examined, her head was shaved, she

00:12:52.930 --> 00:12:59.350
was tattooed with the number 57183RA, and
given a uniform with the inverted red triangle

00:12:59.350 --> 00:13:02.240
that denoted her as a political prisoner.

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:07.990
The waking nightmare of Ravensbruck

00:13:07.990 --> 00:13:12.390
Catherine never freely spoke of her time in
the custody of the Nazis, and it's not surprising.

00:13:12.390 --> 00:13:16.990
The atrocities of Ravensbruck perhaps aren't
as well-known as those of camps like Auschwitz

00:13:16.990 --> 00:13:22.080
— a sad fact that historians say is a result
of the camp's location in what became East

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:26.300
Germany, as well as the fact that it was for
women, and not nearly as well-publicized when

00:13:26.300 --> 00:13:27.300
it was liberated.

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:28.710
But it was no less horrible.

00:13:28.710 --> 00:13:34.190
More than 130,000 people were sent to the
camp, according to best estimates, and surprisingly,

00:13:34.190 --> 00:13:36.620
only about 26,000 were Jewish.

00:13:36.620 --> 00:13:40.720
Others were political prisoners like Catherine,
some were academics deemed too dangerous to

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:44.930
be allowed to speak freely, and some were
simply sent there for not conforming to the

00:13:44.930 --> 00:13:47.630
Nazi ideals of race and feminity.

00:13:47.630 --> 00:13:52.230
Ravensbruck was a work camp, and historians
do know that Catherine worked at a number

00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:56.130
of nearby factories that relied on slave labor
sourced from the camps.

00:13:56.130 --> 00:14:00.300
She made explosives at Torgau, was forced
to labor in a Prussian salt mine, and worked

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:02.020
at a factory building parts for aircraft.

00:14:02.020 --> 00:14:06.510
And she was also part of a group that attempted
to sabotage production in those facilities.

00:14:06.510 --> 00:14:10.780
It's unclear whether or not her subterfuge
was ever discovered, but sabotage was common

00:14:10.780 --> 00:14:14.500
enough that there was a standard punishment
for anyone caught doing it.

00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:19.340
They were sent to the Bunker, a series of
dark and suffocating cells where troublemakers

00:14:19.340 --> 00:14:24.870
would be left to languish away in solitary
confinement… after the beatings and whippings,

00:14:24.870 --> 00:14:27.670
which had been approved by Heinrich Himmler
himself.

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:31.270
Those that were dishing out the punishments
were very good at it — Ravensbruck was the

00:14:31.270 --> 00:14:35.180
training facility for the female guards of
the Nazi war machine, and some of the most

00:14:35.180 --> 00:14:38.230
notorious women of the Third Reich got their
start there.

00:14:38.230 --> 00:14:43.160
Irma Grese, later named the Hyena of Auschwitz,
was already gone by the time Catherine arrived,

00:14:43.160 --> 00:14:47.960
but Dorothea Binz — who would later stand
trial for crimes like murder and setting her

00:14:47.960 --> 00:14:51.040
dogs on the prisoners — was still there.

00:14:51.040 --> 00:14:52.040
Conditions were dismal.

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:55.930
Catherine was just another one of countless
women who were given nothing but rags to wear,

00:14:55.930 --> 00:15:00.270
offered nowhere near enough to eat, and, as
the blizzards and bitter cold winds of winter

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:04.420
descended, it quickly became clear that the
dangerous conditions of the factories and

00:15:04.420 --> 00:15:08.680
their disease-ridden lodgings were not the
only dangers they faced.

00:15:08.680 --> 00:15:13.100
Outbreaks of tuberculosis and dysentery were
common, and so were the medical experiments.

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:17.650
Polish prisoners were known as "rabbits,"
and were often maimed to simulate battlefield

00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:18.650
injuries.

00:15:18.650 --> 00:15:19.650
Most didn't survive.

00:15:19.650 --> 00:15:24.170
A gas chamber was built there in February
of 1945, and thousands had already died there

00:15:24.170 --> 00:15:25.170
by April.

00:15:25.170 --> 00:15:29.230
An adjacent camp, Uckermark, was built for
the old, the ill, the weak, and as an execution

00:15:29.230 --> 00:15:31.720
grounds for those found guilty of espionage.

00:15:31.720 --> 00:15:35.980
Catherine, however, was already gone by the
time her fellow prisoners began to be sent

00:15:35.980 --> 00:15:37.120
to the gas chambers..

00:15:37.120 --> 00:15:40.660
She and a small group of French prisoners
were transferred out of Ravensbruck in February

00:15:40.660 --> 00:15:41.760
of 1945.

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:46.810
They were herded onto freezing cattle trucks,
and stayed there — with no heat, no food,

00:15:46.810 --> 00:15:50.070
and no water — for several days, until they
reached Markkleeberg.

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:55.150
There, they found they were going to be subjected
to an all new sort of hell — hard, outdoor

00:15:55.150 --> 00:15:57.810
work in temperatures that dipped well below
freezing.

00:15:57.810 --> 00:16:03.060
She stayed there for months, until new orders
were handed down on Friday, the 13th of April,

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:04.060
1945.

00:16:04.060 --> 00:16:07.320
It was just weeks before Ravensbruck would
be liberated by a Soviet army who would find

00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:10.610
just around 3,500 prisoners still at the camp.

00:16:10.610 --> 00:16:14.880
The others were all dead or — like Catherine
— told to start walking.

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:19.120
They were under the watchful eye of SS officers
who had simple instructions: shoot anyone

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:20.440
who tried to escape.

00:16:20.440 --> 00:16:24.910
So they walked… and around 115 kilometers
— or about 70 miles — later they came

00:16:24.910 --> 00:16:26.060
to the city of Dresden.

00:16:26.060 --> 00:16:30.740
It lay in ruins; it had been the target of
Allied bombers only a few months before, and

00:16:30.740 --> 00:16:32.950
even today, no one is sure of the death toll.

00:16:32.950 --> 00:16:38.340
Anywhere from 35,000 to 135,000 people were
dead, and the city that had once been called

00:16:38.340 --> 00:16:42.950
"the Florence of the Elbe" had been all but
destroyed by thousands of tons of bombs and

00:16:42.950 --> 00:16:48.270
incendiaries that had set off a firestorm
that engulfed military targets, railways,

00:16:48.270 --> 00:16:51.880
museums, and civilian homes with equal enthusiasm.

00:16:51.880 --> 00:16:55.780
Walking into the smoldering ruins of a city
that had once been one of Europe's most beautiful

00:16:55.780 --> 00:16:57.900
destinations had to be heartbreaking.

00:16:57.900 --> 00:17:02.450
But still, Catherine somehow managed to slip
away from the group, take shelter among the

00:17:02.450 --> 00:17:05.620
ruins, and hide until the SS had marched away.

00:17:05.620 --> 00:17:10.199
On April 19 — a week after she had been
marched out of Markkleeberg — she was found

00:17:10.199 --> 00:17:11.959
by a group of American soldiers.

00:17:11.959 --> 00:17:15.689
She was still near the bombed-out ruins of
Dresden, and when they took her to a hospital

00:17:15.689 --> 00:17:18.589
for care, she would be there for the next
month.

00:17:18.589 --> 00:17:21.929
Meanwhile, Christian had gotten word that
her name had been discovered on a list of

00:17:21.929 --> 00:17:25.909
concentration camp prisoners, but it wasn't
clear where she was, if she had been among

00:17:25.909 --> 00:17:28.539
those liberated… or if she had been among
the dead.

00:17:28.539 --> 00:17:31.990
In an odd twist, he received that message
on April 19 as well.

00:17:31.990 --> 00:17:36.370
For weeks, the only assurances he had that
he would once again see his beloved sister

00:17:36.370 --> 00:17:39.669
came from the clairvoyant he regularly consulted.

00:17:39.669 --> 00:17:44.010
Madame Delahaye had been unwavering in her
insistence that Catherine would return from

00:17:44.010 --> 00:17:48.679
Germany, and it wasn't until May 27 that he
got another message — one that said she

00:17:48.679 --> 00:17:53.139
was, indeed, coming home.

00:17:53.139 --> 00:17:56.490
Homecoming… and immortalization

00:17:56.490 --> 00:18:00.440
Catherine was back home in Paris the day after
her brother got the message telling him that

00:18:00.440 --> 00:18:04.710
she was alive, and she was one of many on
a train of refugees bound for Paris.

00:18:04.710 --> 00:18:09.419
She was a shadow of her former self, emaciated,
unable to eat solid foods, and suffering from

00:18:09.419 --> 00:18:11.120
what we now call PTSD.

00:18:11.120 --> 00:18:15.510
She wasn't just reunited with her brother
— also waiting to greet her was Herve de

00:18:15.510 --> 00:18:19.539
Charbonneries and his children; based on the
accounts of friends, they put their lives

00:18:19.539 --> 00:18:23.909
back together, built a family of their own,
and Catherine was allowed to live on in something

00:18:23.909 --> 00:18:26.259
they described as "relative peace."

00:18:26.259 --> 00:18:30.179
While she rarely talked about what she went
through during her imprisonment, she did testify

00:18:30.179 --> 00:18:35.940
at a military tribunal in 1952 and as a strange
sort of footnote to that, Friedrich Berger

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:41.149
didn't stand trial for his actions in breaking
up the French Resistance by torture and murder.

00:18:41.149 --> 00:18:45.519
He had been captured by British forces, was
eventually handed over to the Americans, and

00:18:45.519 --> 00:18:47.350
went on to work with the secret service.

00:18:47.350 --> 00:18:51.340
As for Catherine, her work in the Resistance
and her bravery in the face of torture was

00:18:51.340 --> 00:18:52.340
recognized.

00:18:52.340 --> 00:18:56.360
She was awarded a slew of medals, including
the Croix de Guerre, the Combatant Volunteer

00:18:56.360 --> 00:19:00.720
Cross of the Resistance, the Combatant Cross,
and — from Britain — the King's Medal

00:19:00.720 --> 00:19:02.799
for Courage in the Cause of Freedom.

00:19:02.799 --> 00:19:07.110
She was also named a chevaliere of the Legion
d'Honneur, and, of course, Christian would

00:19:07.110 --> 00:19:11.660
name his first perfume for her just two years
after her return to Paris.

00:19:11.660 --> 00:19:12.980
And, she stayed busy.

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:17.070
She became a representative in cut flowers
and for more than a decade, she and Herve

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:22.399
would arrive at the Hallas market at 4am to
set up their display of cut flowers brought

00:19:22.399 --> 00:19:24.889
into the City of Lights from all over the
world.

00:19:24.889 --> 00:19:30.169
She and Herve lived together until his death
in 1985, and for years, she faithfully attended

00:19:30.169 --> 00:19:34.160
an annual memorial service for members of
the French Resistance.

00:19:34.160 --> 00:19:38.509
Even throughout her later years, she remained
steadfast in her support for the cause she

00:19:38.509 --> 00:19:42.850
had nearly given her life for: She publically
distanced herself from the more controversial

00:19:42.850 --> 00:19:46.590
members of her family, including her niece
Francoise, who just as publically declared

00:19:46.590 --> 00:19:51.620
herself a neo-Nazi when she married British
extremist leader Colin Jordan in a ceremony

00:19:51.620 --> 00:19:55.000
that involved a copy of Hitler's Mein Kampf.

00:19:55.000 --> 00:19:59.740
Christian Dior died in 1957, and afterwards,
Herve and Catherine moved back to Callian,

00:19:59.740 --> 00:20:01.430
where it had all started.

00:20:01.430 --> 00:20:06.269
She grew roses destined for inclusion in the
perfume her brother had named for her, and

00:20:06.269 --> 00:20:10.929
went on to help establish the Dior Museum
in the Granville home they had grown up in.

00:20:10.929 --> 00:20:27.860
She never returned to Germany, and passed
away in 2008.

